Q: How to preserve interim filename extensions when specifying `output.filename` in Webpack? A: <p><code>[base].js</code> is literal here because <code>output.filename</code> is based on the entry name, not on the source file basename. Set entry names to the desired values and keep <code>output.filename: '[name].js'</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// webpack.config.js
const path = require('path');

module.exports = {
  entry: {
    'library.global': './src/library.global.ts',
    'library.module': './src/library.module.ts',
    'main.local.en': './src/main.local.en.ts',
    'main.prod.ja': './src/main.prod.ja.ts',
  },
  output: {
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),
    filename: '[name].js',
  },
};
</code></pre>
<p>That emits:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>library.global.js
library.module.js
main.local.en.js
main.prod.ja.js
</code></pre>
<p>Webpack documents <code>output.filename</code> substitutions such as <a href="https://webpack.js.org/configuration/output/#filename" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>[name]</code></a>; with object <code>entry</code> syntax, object keys are the entry names. In this configuration, <code>[name]</code> is <code>library.global</code>, <code>library.module</code>, <code>main.local.en</code>, etc.</p>
<p>If entries are discovered dynamically, generate an object whose keys strip only the last extension (<code>.ts</code>, <code>.coffee</code>, etc.), not everything after the first dot.</p>
